1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,696
This lesson represents all six narrative sections in school 5A. The
emphasis is on actions that fit a paramedic encounter: notice a
barrier, ask what matters, explain choices and connect the person to
an appropriate next step.

2
00:00:15,696 --> 00:00:29,040
Advocacy does not mean choosing for someone or promising access to a
service. It means helping a person understand options, supporting
their stated goal and making a useful handover or referral when
possible.

3
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:44,328
Social determinants of health are conditions in which people are born,
grow, live, work and age, along with the wider forces shaping those
conditions. They can affect exposure to risk and access to protection
or care.

4
00:00:44,328 --> 00:00:55,488
A barrier is not a personal failure. Ask what made the current plan
hard to follow and what support would make the next step realistic,
safe and acceptable to the patient.

5
00:00:55,488 --> 00:01:10,464
The school names five domains. Use them to identify a relevant
question, not to interview every patient about every social detail. A
single observation does not establish the whole household’s
circumstances.

6
00:01:10,464 --> 00:01:24,168
Economic stability and health-care access can interact: an appointment
may exist but the trip, medication cost or communication arrangement
may be unusable. Ask what prevents this specific next step.

7
00:01:24,168 --> 00:01:38,688
Education, the built environment and social context add different
questions about usable information, the physical route and wanted
support. Do not infer literacy, reliability or available family help
from appearance.

8
00:01:38,688 --> 00:01:56,496
Structural racism and colonial policies can create barriers beyond an
individual encounter. Recognize the reported barrier, avoid
discriminatory assumptions and use an appropriate patient-chosen
advocacy route; respectful words alone do not remove a system barrier.

9
00:01:56,496 --> 00:02:09,240
A missed appointment, medication gap or repeated 911 call may signal a
mismatch between the care plan and real life. It does not by itself
show a lack of interest or responsibility.

10
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:22,584
Use intersectionality as a prompt to ask how systems, identity,
geography and resources overlap in this encounter. Do not impose a
theory on the patient or assume that a group shares one experience.

11
00:02:22,584 --> 00:02:37,872
Use one idea at a time, familiar words, short sentences and concrete
next steps. Avoid unexplained abbreviations and jargon, especially
when a person is in pain, frightened, tired or using a second
language.

12
00:02:37,872 --> 00:02:51,600
A clear explanation includes the concern, what you recommend, the
reasonable alternatives, what could happen if care is declined and how
the person can seek help later. Confirm that the person had a chance
to ask questions.

13
00:02:51,600 --> 00:03:05,112
An instruction can fail before anyone reads it. The patient may not
have the link, device, language support or vision needed to obtain it.
A leaflet handed over is not proof that usable information was
delivered.

14
00:03:05,112 --> 00:03:19,296
Explain one important action in ordinary words, then ask for the
person’s own explanation or demonstration. A correct technical word
repeated back can coexist with misunderstanding of the actual next
step.

15
00:03:19,296 --> 00:03:32,808
If information conflicts, identify the source of the confusion and
clarify the current plan within your role. Do not dismiss the person’s
question as low literacy or substitute an unapproved medication
change.

16
00:03:32,808 --> 00:03:44,040
Finish by checking whether the plan can be used with the resources
available. Understanding an appointment instruction and having a safe
way to reach the appointment are separate achievements.

17
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:54,960
Teach-back is a check on the clarity of your communication. Use a
supportive tone and ask the person to describe the plan in their own
words; do not frame it as a test they can fail.

18
00:03:54,960 --> 00:04:08,520
If the answer is unclear, revise your explanation. Ask about the
preferred language, hearing, vision, memory, pain or distraction, and
offer appropriate communication support before making assumptions.

19
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:21,576
Prevention can include a clear symptom action plan, a safer
environment, connection to a support service or follow-up with the
patient’s existing team. It should fit the person’s goals and the
paramedic’s role.

20
00:04:21,576 --> 00:04:31,296
Avoid blame and fear-based teaching. A small plan the person accepts
is more useful than a long plan that requires resources they do not
have.

21
00:04:31,296 --> 00:04:44,280
Primary prevention acts before the first event. A role-appropriate
discussion of safe storage or an environmental injury risk can help
reduce the chance of harm, without claiming you can eliminate the
risk.

22
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:59,664
Secondary prevention aims at early recognition and follow-up. An
approved screening programme is useful only when a concerning result
leads to the right next assessment. A screening result is not
automatically a diagnosis.

23
00:04:59,664 --> 00:05:13,824
Tertiary prevention concerns established illness and its complications
or functional effects. Support for an existing care plan,
rehabilitation access or avoiding a repeated complication can fit this
goal.

24
00:05:13,824 --> 00:05:26,184
The same type of activity can serve different goals. Explain the
intended outcome and stage of the problem rather than memorizing one
service as permanently primary, secondary or tertiary.

25
00:05:26,184 --> 00:05:40,416
In an original teaching example, an adult using a mobility aid reports
that a broken building lift makes the planned route unusable. First
ask what is true today; do not assume the patient has been unwilling
to attend care.

26
00:05:40,416 --> 00:05:53,184
Before any new injury, identifying a safer feasible route or
appropriate building support can serve primary prevention. Do not
prescribe untrained stair carrying as a solution to the access
problem.

27
00:05:53,184 --> 00:06:05,784
If the encounter reveals a new clinical concern, early appropriate
assessment serves a secondary-prevention goal. It does not become a
social-navigation-only call because an access barrier was found.

28
00:06:05,784 --> 00:06:20,568
For an established condition, helping the patient reconnect with the
existing care plan may reduce complications. Confirm an actual next
step and a fallback; do not promise a service or transport arrangement
that has not been accepted.

29
00:06:20,568 --> 00:06:37,944
BC 211 can help people find community resources such as housing, food,
mental-health or social supports. HealthLink BC 8-1-1 offers non-
emergency health information and navigation. These services do not
replace emergency response.

30
00:06:37,944 --> 00:06:50,856
Offer the option, explain its purpose and ask permission before
sharing information or placing a call. A referral is useful only if
the service is open, accessible and acceptable to the person.

31
00:06:50,856 --> 00:07:08,712
The school narrative says nurses and pharmacists at HealthLink are
both available around the clock. The current official page
distinguishes them: navigation and nursing are available continuously,
while pharmacist availability is five in the evening to nine in the
morning, seven days a week.

32
00:07:08,712 --> 00:07:23,064
The BC Two-One-One page currently lists navigator contact from nine in
the morning to nine in the evening on weekdays, excluding holidays. A
searchable directory and a live navigator are not the same access
channel.

33
00:07:23,064 --> 00:07:36,408
These are date-checked resource details, not a promise for every
future call. Confirm current hours, language or communication access
and the service the person actually needs before relying on the
option.

34
00:07:36,408 --> 00:07:55,968
The school also names Indigenous supports, harm-reduction services,
crisis support and community paramedicine. Check local eligibility and
referral routes; do not assume each programme is available everywhere.
None replaces the immediate emergency response or assessment that the
situation requires.

35
00:07:55,968 --> 00:08:10,032
An original offer could be: You said the route is the obstacle. Would
you like us to explore an approved service that may help with access?
A no should lead to clarification of the patient’s wishes, not
pressure or a punitive label.

36
00:08:10,032 --> 00:08:21,528
Explain what information would be shared and why. Obtain the
appropriate permission and use the approved channel. Do not place a
referral merely because you have learned a private detail.

37
00:08:21,528 --> 00:08:37,272
A warm handoff actively links the person and receiving service when
feasible. Confirm whether the receiving team accepted the request or
whether the established process has been followed. Leaving a message
is not the same as a confirmed appointment.

38
00:08:37,272 --> 00:08:52,536
Tell the patient what is confirmed, what remains pending and what to
do if the connection fails. Document the actual outcome, including a
declined or unavailable option, without calling it successful just
because a number was provided.

39
00:08:52,536 --> 00:09:11,568
If a patient declines transport, follow the current BCEHS A09 process
and the applicable law. Assess the situation, support communication,
explore concerns, explain risks and alternatives, consult as required
and document the patient’s decision and plan.

40
00:09:11,568 --> 00:09:27,960
A signature alone does not make a refusal safe or informed. A
clinician’s disagreement, an unusual choice or a diagnosis alone does
not prove incapacity. Do not leave a person with a plan they cannot
access without exploring an alternative.

41
00:09:27,960 --> 00:09:41,136
A directory result answers where a service might exist; it does not
decide whether today’s patient can safely use it instead of transport.
Clinical assessment and the person’s informed decision remain central.

42
00:09:41,136 --> 00:09:58,584
Current A09 distinguishes a refusal from an appropriate alternative
pathway. It requires the alternative to be suitable, available and
understood. Clinical uncertainty, red flags or concern about follow-
through call for transport encouragement and consultation.

43
00:09:58,584 --> 00:10:14,592
A09 requires CliniCall before non-conveyance for children and people
in police custody, regardless of condition. Do not let a reassuring
appearance or a signed form erase a required consultation or a limited
ability to choose freely.

44
00:10:14,592 --> 00:10:30,984
The close must include a specific contingency the person can use.
Confirm understanding and access, not simply that advice was spoken.
If a serious problem remains unresolved, escalate rather than
substituting a community number for care.

45
00:10:30,984 --> 00:10:43,128
Palliative care focuses on quality of life, symptom relief and the
person’s goals. It does not mean “nothing more can be done,” and it
does not automatically mean the patient should stay home.

46
00:10:43,128 --> 00:10:58,320
Ask the patient what matters now, identify any acute change, verify
applicable care-goal documents through the current pathway and
coordinate with the patient’s team when appropriate. Use only
interventions within your authority and training.

47
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:12,000
A useful record distinguishes what you observed from what the patient,
family or bystander reported. Use quotations selectively and attribute
them. Do not use a stigmatizing label as a substitute for behavior.

48
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:26,688
Record the communication support offered, teach-back or other check of
understanding, practical barrier identified, options discussed,
consultation and contingency plan. Record only what is relevant under
the service policy.

49
00:11:26,688 --> 00:11:39,792
Subjective means information reported by an identified source. It is
not a synonym for unreliable. Keep relevant patient words and
distinguish their account from a relative’s or bystander’s statement.

50
00:11:39,792 --> 00:11:52,728
Objective means observations and measurements you actually obtained.
Do not move a patient-reported service barrier into this column as
though you inspected it, and do not invent normal findings to complete
the format.

51
00:11:52,728 --> 00:12:06,720
Assessment states the interpretation and its uncertainty. Social risk
can contribute to a problem without proving its medical cause. Avoid
turning an access barrier into a diagnosis or a blame statement.

52
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:25,800
Plan records the action and outcome: teaching and response, options
and decision, actual referral status, consultation, safety net and
responsibility. SOAP is an organizer, not a replacement for required
ePCR fields or an excuse to omit the clinical assessment.

53
00:12:25,800 --> 00:12:41,352
This original excerpt concerns the mobility-access example. In
Subjective: The patient reports missing a follow-up visit because the
building lift is not working. Attribute the statement instead of
assuming you verified the entire account.

54
00:12:41,352 --> 00:12:58,896
For Objective, record what was actually observed or measured in the
encounter. If the crew has not inspected the lift, do not chart
confirmed lift failure. The complete clinical findings belong in the
actual assessment record, not invented numbers in this exercise.

55
00:12:58,896 --> 00:13:13,368
For Assessment: Reported access barrier may affect follow-up; further
clinical and practical assessment determines the safe plan. Do not
write that all symptoms are caused by missed care solely because a
social obstacle is present.

56
00:13:13,368 --> 00:13:32,472
For Plan, distinguish the actual statuses: permission given for a
particular contact, request sent through the approved route,
acceptance pending, and a discussed fallback. This is a documentation
excerpt, not a complete non-conveyance record or authority to leave
the patient.

57
00:13:32,472 --> 00:13:44,400
An older adult receiving palliative care asks for help with worsening
breathlessness and says, “I do not want to go back to hospital.” A
family member says, “There is nothing you can do.”

58
00:13:44,400 --> 00:14:02,184
Assess the new symptom and immediate risk, ask the patient’s
priorities, explain available options and limits, verify current care-
goal documents and consult the appropriate service pathway. Respect
the patient’s role; do not substitute the family member’s preference.

59
00:14:02,184 --> 00:14:12,336
A good plan is not just medically reasonable. It should also be
understandable, feasible, culturally safer and connected to the
person’s stated priorities.

60
00:14:12,336 --> 00:14:25,128
Use this four-question check before closing a consultation or
transfer. If a serious risk remains, escalate through the current
service pathway rather than relying on a community referral alone.

61
00:14:25,128 --> 00:14:39,408
Before closing, ask the person to explain the next step in their own
words. Listen for a practical obstacle as well as a misunderstanding.
A correctly repeated phone number does not prove the person can place
a call.

62
00:14:39,408 --> 00:14:53,568
Name who is doing what and which parts have actually been agreed. If
another team has not accepted the request, say pending. If the patient
declines the option, record that choice and the rest of the clinically
appropriate plan.

63
00:14:53,568 --> 00:15:06,744
Discuss what would make the person seek help sooner and where
appropriate help can be reached. Match the contingency to the assessed
problem; do not use a generic list to hide unresolved clinical
uncertainty.

64
00:15:06,744 --> 00:15:21,144
Check the plan once more against the patient’s stated goal,
understanding, access and current risk. Advocacy is demonstrated by a
usable connection and honest limits, not by the number of resource
names supplied.
